The diffuse neutrino flux is expected to consist of astrophysical neutrinos, produced through interactions inside their sources, and cosmogenic neutrinos, produced during extragalactic propagation. Astrophysical neutrinos are generated both hadronically and photohadronically within or near their sources. Cosmogenic neutrinos are produced through photopion production interactions of UHECRs with the cosmic microwave background (CMB) and extragalactic background light (EBL). In particular, the proton flux above about 1019.710^{19.7} eV is suppressed by these interactions with the CMB via the Greisen–Zatsepin–Kuzmin (GZK) effect [41, 59]. Therefore, the total diffuse UHE​ν\mathrm{UHE}
u flux provides a probe of both the chemical composition of UHECRs and the conditions inside their sources.

